Part as a fraction of the whole $=\dfrac{\text{that part}}{\text{total number of parts}}$.

[tikz]
% mathsmap-diagram-colours {"version":1,"kind":"geometry","base":[],"semantic":[{"name":"ratioBlueFill","hex":"D4E8FF","reason":"Blue parts distinguish the selected ratio group."}],"reference":"Existing mathematical regions, counts and geometry retained; standard palette; ordinary outlines black"}
\definecolor{ratioBlueFill}{HTML}{D4E8FF}
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\foreach \x in {0,0.8} {\draw[fill=ratioBlueFill] (\x,0) rectangle ++(0.8,0.8);}
\foreach \x in {1.6,2.4,3.2} {\draw[fill=white] (\x,0) rectangle ++(0.8,0.8);}
\draw[decorate,decoration={brace,mirror,amplitude=6pt}] (0,-0.15) -- (1.6,-0.15) node[midway,below=10pt] {$m$};
\draw[decorate,decoration={brace,amplitude=6pt}] (0,0.95) -- (4,0.95) node[midway,above=10pt] {$m+n$};
\node at (2,-1.6) {$\dfrac{m}{m+n}$ of the whole};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

Part as a fraction of another part $=\dfrac{\text{that part}}{\text{the other part}}$, which can exceed $1$: in $3:2$ the first part is $\frac{3}{2}$ of the second.

