workedExamples.solution_text:
There are $3$ shaded cells and $5$ unshaded cells, making $3+5=8$ cells altogether.
[tikz]% mathsmap-diagram-colours {"version":1,"kind":"geometry","base":[],"semantic":[{"name":"templateShade","hex":"D4E8FF","reason":"Retains the question's three shaded and five unshaded cells; the solution prose supplies the derived counts."}],"reference":"template ratio-fraction-grid-solution"}
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=1mm,y=1mm]
\special{dvisvgm:raw <metadata data-graph-strokes="1"/>}
\definecolor{templateShade}{HTML}{D4E8FF}
\path[use as bounding box] (-0.6,-12.6) rectangle (20.6,0.6);
\fill[templateShade] (0,-6) rectangle (5,0);
\fill[templateShade] (5,-6) rectangle (10,0);
\fill[templateShade] (0,-12) rectangle (5,-6);
\draw[black,line width=0.4pt] (5,0)--(5,-12);
\draw[black,line width=0.4pt] (10,0)--(10,-12);
\draw[black,line width=0.4pt] (15,0)--(15,-12);
\draw[black,line width=0.4pt] (0,-6)--(20,-6);
\draw[black,line width=0.8pt] (0,0) rectangle (20,-12);
\end{tikzpicture}[/tikz]
a. The shaded fraction of the whole is $\dfrac{3}{8}$.
b. The unshaded fraction of the whole is $\dfrac{5}{8}$.
c. Alex used the number of unshaded cells as the denominator instead of the total. The number of shaded cells is $\dfrac{3}{5}$ of the number of unshaded cells.

